CLAIM EXTRACTION / INFLATION MAP
================================
Target: Mann, M.E., Bradley, R.S. & Hughes, M.K. (1998), "Global-scale temperature
patterns and climate forcing over the past six centuries," Nature 392, 779-787.
(hereafter MBH98)

Document type: academic paper (multiproxy paleoclimate reconstruction)

Source verification: FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED. Full text retrieved and read from
meteo.psu.edu/holocene/.../mbh98.pdf on run date.

The claim that prompted this audit (as encountered): the "hockey stick" —
commonly restated as "1998 was the warmest year in 1,000 years / of the
millennium." This is recorded as the inflated form on purpose; part of the map's
job is to determine whether MBH98 actually said it.

-----------------------------------------------------------------
STAGE NOTE
-----------------------------------------------------------------
This stage extracts and maps. It does NOT rule on whether MBH98's method
supports its own quantified claim — that is the prosecutors' job. A documented,
still-contested methodological literature exists (McIntyre & McKitrick 2003/2005;
NRC/North Report 2006; Wahl & Ammann 2007; Huybers 2005) and is noted where
relevant as reception context only, never adjudicated here.

=================================================================
1. WHAT WAS LITERALLY MEASURED
=================================================================
Instrument / data source: a multiproxy network of annual-resolution indicators —
dendroclimatic (tree-ring), ice-core, ice-melt, and coral records, plus long
historical and instrumental series. 112 indicators back to 1820, declining with
age to 22 indicators back to 1400.

Calibration / verification: proxies calibrated against gridded instrumental land
air + sea-surface temperature, 1902-1980 (training). Verified against a withheld
1854-1901 gridded subset (219 grid-points) and 11 long instrumental series.

Method: principal component analysis of the instrumental temperature field to
isolate leading eigenvectors; each proxy calibrated against the leading PCs
during 1902-80; an overdetermined inverse solution then reconstructs the PCs
(RPCs) year-by-year before the calibration period. Hemispheric/global means,
a NINO3 index, and gridded spatial patterns are derived from the RPCs.

Population / unit of analysis: Northern Hemisphere (and global) mean annual
surface-temperature anomaly (deg C, relative to 1902-80), and gridded spatial
anomaly patterns, reconstructed back to AD 1400 (i.e., "six centuries" / ~600
years), with error bars that expand back in time.

Scope caveat stated IN the source (not added here): reconstructive skill degrades
with age. Back to 1400 the network "resolves only the first eigenvector"
(~40-50% of NH variance in calibration/verification); spatial reconstructions are
"of marginal usefulness" before ~1600; "useful reconstruction" terminates at AD
1400. The deep-time NH claim therefore rests on a single resolved eigenvector.

Verification state: FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED.

=================================================================
2. STRONGEST CLAIM THE SOURCE ITSELF MAKES (hedges preserved)
=================================================================
Abstract (qualitative form): "Northern Hemisphere mean annual temperatures for
three of the past eight years are warmer than any other year since (at least)
AD 1400."  [Hedge preserved: "(at least)".]

Results (quantified form — the version Rule 2 targets): "Taking into account the
uncertainties in our NH reconstruction ... it appears that the years 1990, 1995
and now 1997 ... each show anomalies that are greater than any other year back to
1400 at 3 standard errors, or roughly a 99.7% level of certainty."
[Hedges preserved: "Taking into account the uncertainties," "it appears that."]

Note on within-source framing: the abstract states the exceedance qualitatively
but keeps BOTH the 1400 bound and a hedge; the quantified 99.7% figure lives in
the Results. The abstract does NOT extend the scope beyond what the body
supports (contrast the common pattern of methods-hedged / abstract-inflated).

A DISTINCT second claim also appears (not the target of this map): an attribution
claim — "greenhouse gases emerging as the dominant forcing during the twentieth
century" / "It is reasonable to infer that greenhouse-gas forcing is now the
dominant external forcing." [Hedge preserved: "reasonable to infer."] Per Rule 1
(one claim only), this is flagged, not audited here. Auditing it requires a
second run.

Location of claim: abstract + Results section. Verification state:
FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED.

=================================================================
3. WEAKER DEFENSIBLE CLAIM THE EVIDENCE ACTUALLY SUPPORTS
=================================================================
Within THIS multiproxy reconstruction — calibrated on 1902-80, cross-validated
against 1854-1901, and carrying uncertainties that widen back in time and rest on
a single resolved eigenvector before ~1600 — the recent instrumental NH annual-
mean values (1990, 1995, 1997) fall outside the reconstructed range (at the
paper's stated 3-standard-error level) for any year back to AD 1400.

Scope limits the measurement can carry:
  - Northern Hemisphere, annual mean (NOT global; NOT a specific calendar-day or
    seasonal claim).
  - Bounded at AD 1400 / ~600 years (NOT "the millennium," NOT "ever").
  - Conditional on this proxy network, this method, and this stated error model —
    it is a statement about exceeding THIS reconstruction, not an unconditional
    statement about all past temperatures.

Verification state: author reconstruction from Field 1.

This field is the reference point. Gap #2->#3 is any paper-level overclaim;
gap #3->#4 is downstream inflation. They are different problems.

=================================================================
4. BROADER CLAIM ATTACHED DOWNSTREAM
=================================================================
The broadest restatement in circulation is the millennial one: "1998 the warmest
year of the millennium / warmest in 1,000 years." The attribution check below is
run on it before it is allowed to anchor anything.

--- RETRIEVED (assessment/policy layer) ---
IPCC Third Assessment Report (2001), WGI Summary for Policymakers:
"it is likely that the 1990s have been the warmest decade and 1998 the warmest
year of the millennium. [Based upon (a) Chapter 2, Figure 2.7c and (b) Chapter 2,
Figure 2.20]"
Source located: IPCC WGI TAR SPM (via ResearchGate copy of the report and an
arXiv statistics paper reproducing the SPM sentence and figure caption).
The figure it rests on (the 1000-year NH reconstruction, IPCC Fig. 2.20 / the SPM
"past 1000 years" curve) is captioned to Mann, Bradley & Hughes 1999.

--- RETRIEVED (the actual supporting successor source) ---
Mann, Bradley & Hughes (1999), "Northern hemisphere temperatures during the past
millennium: inferences, uncertainties, and limitations," Geophys. Res. Lett. 26,
759-762 (MBH99). Abstract: "our results suggest that the latter 20th century is
anomalous in the context of at least the past millennium. The 1990s was the
warmest decade, and 1998 the warmest year, at moderately high levels of
confidence ... Though expanded uncertainties prevent decisive conclusions for the
period prior to AD 1400."
MBH99 is the source that reaches 1,000 years; it explicitly extends MBH98's
method to new/older data and states its own (heavier) uncertainties.

--- RETRIEVED (journal-highlight layer, downstream of MBH99 not MBH98) ---
GRL vol. 26 no. 6 "Highlights" for MBH99: headline "Year 1998 is the warmest in a
millennium." This flattens MBH99's "at moderately high levels of confidence" into
a flat headline — but it is a restatement of MBH99, not MBH98.

--- RETRIEVED (PR/policy echo) ---
Popular/policy restatements ("1998 was the warmest year of the millennium in the
Northern Hemisphere" — e.g., 2001 Globe and Mail coverage and a Canadian Kyoto
promotional pamphlet, as quoted in McKitrick's 2005 conference paper) trace to
the IPCC TAR SPM sentence above, which rests on the MBH99 figure. None of the
retrieved popular restatements pin the millennial claim to the 1998 paper
specifically.

--- NONE FOUND ---
Searched for a downstream text that cites MBH98 (the 1998 paper) SPECIFICALLY as
the authority for the 1,000-year / "warmest of the millennium" claim. Searches
run: "IPCC TAR 2001 hockey stick MBH98 MBH99 1000 years citation"; "IPCC TAR
1998 warmest year millennium chapter 2 Mann 1999 citation." Found none — the
retrieved attributions for the millennial claim point to MBH99, correctly.

ATTRIBUTION CHECK (run on the millennial restatement):
  Q1 - Could MBH98 alone support "warmest year of the millennium / past 1,000
       years"? NO. Scope arithmetic: MBH98 reaches AD 1400 (~600 years); the
       restatement asserts 1,000 years. The extra 400 years is carried by
       something other than MBH98.
  Q2 - Who does the downstream text cite for it? IPCC TAR's millennial figure and
       sentence rest on Mann, Bradley & Hughes 1999. That is the ACTUAL
       supporting source, cited/figured correctly.
  => ATTRIBUTED ELSEWHERE. The millennial claim rests on MBH99 and the downstream
     assessment attributes it to MBH99. This is NOT inflation by MBH98, and NOT a
     misattribution against MBH98. (Where IPCC cites the pair jointly, the report
     and the NRC/North Report distinguish their spans explicitly — MBH98 = 600
     yr, MBH99 = 1,000 yr — which makes any joint citation SYNTHESIS ATTRIBUTION,
     not misattribution.)

Verification state for the attribution relationship: CROSS-SOURCE-CONFIRMED
(read the downstream IPCC text, the audited source MBH98, and the successor MBH99,
and compared what each supports).

=================================================================
5. WHERE THE INFLATION BEGINS
=================================================================
ANCHORING CHECK: the classification below rests on SUPPLIED/RETRIEVED items only
(IPCC TAR SPM sentence — RETRIEVED; MBH99 abstract — RETRIEVED; MBH98 full text —
FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED). No RECALLED item anchors this field.

WITHIN-SOURCE component (MBH98 alone; established from the paper, NOT provisional):
No clear within-source scope inflation. The abstract preserves the 1400 bound and
a hedge ("(at least)"); the quantified 99.7% claim sits in Results with its own
hedges ("it appears that," "Taking into account the uncertainties"). The abstract
does not stretch scope beyond the body. (Whether the 3-SE / 99.7% figure is itself
warranted given single-eigenvector resolution back to 1400 and the off-centered
PCA later flagged by M&M and partially acknowledged by NRC 2006 is a
CONSTRUCT/INTERNAL-VALIDITY question for the prosecutors, not a
measurement->claim->downstream gap. The map locates it; it does not rule on it.)

DOWNSTREAM component (the millennial restatement):
The transition "greater than any year back to 1400 / ~600 years" -> "warmest year
of the millennium / past 1,000 years" is a SCOPE EXTENSION. Per Field 4 it is
carried out BY MBH99 — a same-author successor with its own older data and its own
(heavier) uncertainty statements — and is ATTRIBUTED to MBH99 in IPCC TAR. A
same-author successor is still a different source; treating MBH99's millennial
claim as an inflation of MBH98 would be a category error, and the downstream text
does not cite MBH98 alone for it.

A separate hedge-stripping does occur downstream — MBH99's "moderately high levels
of confidence" / "expanded uncertainties prevent decisive conclusions prior to AD
1400" becomes the flat GRL headline "1998 is the warmest in a millennium" and the
IPCC's calibrated "likely." But that is inflation of MBH99, outside this map's
target.

FAILURE LOCATION for THIS map's target claim (MBH98's own 1400-bounded quantified
claim): the claim LIVES in the PAPER; there is no established downstream inflation
of it. The millennial inflation that the public associates with "the hockey stick"
belongs to a DIFFERENT source (MBH99) and its communication layer — classified
ATTRIBUTED ELSEWHERE with respect to MBH98.

Verification state: CROSS-SOURCE-CONFIRMED.

=================================================================
6. WHICH CLAIM IS THIS AUDIT EVALUATING?
=================================================================
Rule 2 applied — the most precisely quantified version is targeted, and it is
MBH98's own claim (#2), not the downstream restatement:

"In the MBH98 reconstruction, Northern Hemisphere mean annual temperature
anomalies for 1990, 1995 and 1997 exceed those of any year back to AD 1400 at
three standard errors (roughly 99.7% certainty)."

This audits the SOURCE'S OWN claim (tests whether MBH98 overreached in its own
quantified statement). It deliberately does NOT target the downstream "warmest of
the millennium" restatement, because that restatement is carried by MBH99 and
attributed to MBH99 — findings about it would be findings about MBH99 and the
assessment/communication layer, not about MBH98.

(The MBH98 forcing/attribution claim in Field 2 is a distinct claim with a
different evidence base; if you want it audited, run the sequence a second time
with that claim in Field 6.)

